Bus-master DMA for the hard disks: the PIIX3 IDE function gets its bus master (prog-if 0x80, BAR4: command,
status and PRD table pointer for each channel), READ DMA / WRITE DMA (C8-CB), SET FEATURES (transfer mode,
multiword DMA 0-2 as a PIIX3's; the rest accepted) and IDENTIFY's DMA words. A DMA command waits for the bus
master's start (or runs at once if it was started first), then moves its sectors between the image and guest RAM
by the PRD table, up to 128 sectors at a time through the drive's buffer, and interrupts once: no PIO a sector at a
time. Guest addresses outside RAM or in the 640K-1M hole are refused (an error, as a bus fault). Writes into guest
RAM are reported through T386_DMA_WRITE (as i8257.c's, patch 0005) for a code translator. IDETIM's decode bits are
set as a BIOS leaves them (Linux's ata_piix skips a channel without them), and so are BAR4 (0xc000) and I/O
decoding and bus mastering (Linux's ata_generic takes only an enabled controller). The drive's buffer is whole
cache lines (posix_memalign), so that the image's reads and writes can go straight to and from it. CD-ROM drives
stay PIO (they accept a PIO transfer mode). The BIOS and DOS keep using PIO.

